concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "14L How seized things must be dealt with"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-14l ;
    values:originalText "Seizure notice to be served (1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing seized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day, serve a seizure notice on: (a) the owner of the thing; or (b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if: (a) both: (i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries; and (ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or (b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served under subsection (1). (3) A seizure notice must: (a) identify the thing; and (b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and (c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized; and" ;
    skos:definition "Seizure notice to be served (1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing seized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day, serve a seizure notice on: (a) the owner of the thing; or (b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if: (a) both: (i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries; and (ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or (b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served under subsection (1). (3) A seizure notice must: (a) identify the thing; and (b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and (c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized; and"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "0da13b6e3e0319356b5ff773083704ef5d8ba92e58327022548903343c25a340" ;
    cof:pageNumber "49"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-14l-ss-3 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf> ;
    dc:source "australian-federal-police-act-1979.pdf, p.49" .
doc:sec-14l a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "14L How seized things must be dealt with" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "Seizure notice to be served (1) A police officer who is for the time being responsible for a thing seized under section 14K must, within 7 days after the seizure day, serve a seizure notice on: (a) the owner of the thing; or (b) if the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries—the person from whom the thing was seized. (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if: (a) both: (i) the owner of the thing cannot be identified after reasonable inquiries; and (ii) the thing was not seized from a person; or (b) it is not possible to serve the person required to be served under subsection (1). (3) A seizure notice must: (a) identify the thing; and (b) state the date on which the thing was seized; and (c) state the ground or grounds on which the thing was seized; and" ;
    cof:pageNumber "49"^^xsd:integer .

