concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49m a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "49M Contravention of restraining orders"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sec-49m ;
    values:originalText "(1) A person who intentionally contravenes a restraining order by disposing of, or otherwise dealing with, property that is subject to the restraining order commits an offence punishable, upon conviction, by imprisonment for a period not exceeding 5 years. (2) Where: (a) a restraining order is made against property; and (b) the property is disposed of, or otherwise dealt with, in contravention of the restraining order; and   Loss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA Restraining orders  Division 3A Section 49N Australian Federal Police Act 1979 Compilation date: 08/11/2025 (c) the disposition or dealing was either not for sufficient consideration or not in favour of a person who acted in good faith; the DPP may apply to the court that made the restraining order for an order that the disposition or dealing be set aside. (3) Where the DPP makes an application under subsection (2) in relation to a disposition or dealing, the court may make an order: (a) setting the disposition or dealing aside as from the day on which it took place; or (b) setting the disposition or dealing aside as from the day of the order under this subsection and declaring the respective rights of any persons who acquired interests in the property on or after the day on which the disposition or dealing took place and before the day of the order under this subsection." ;
    skos:definition "(1) A person who intentionally contravenes a restraining order by disposing of, or otherwise dealing with, property that is subject to the restraining order commits an offence punishable, upon conviction, by imprisonment for a period not exceeding 5 years. (2) Where: (a) a restraining order is made against property; and (b) the property is disposed of, or otherwise dealt with, in contravention of the restraining order; and   Loss of certain superannuation rights and benefits  Part VA Restraining orders  Division 3A Section 49N Australian Federal Police Act 1979 Compilation date: 08/11/2025 (c) the disposition or dealing was either not for sufficient consideration or not in favour of a person who acted in good faith; the DPP may apply to the court that made the restraining order for an order that the disposition or dealing be set aside. (3) Where the DPP makes an application under subsection (2) in relation to a disposition or dealing, the court may make an order: (a) setting the disposition or dealing aside as from the day on which it took place; or (b) setting the disposition or dealing aside as from the day of the order under this subsection and declaring the respective rights of any persons who acquired interests in the property on or after the day on which the disposition or dealing took place and before the day of the order under this subsection."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "edb03f01a84bb40160f6c037b6cdfdb019ba0527811a2b0883fe925780f4f614" ;
    cof:pageNumber "174"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49m-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49m-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:australian-federal-police-act-1979-sec-49m-ss-3 ;
